{
  "id": "Compound::DB01236 - Gene::2567",
  "verbalization": "Sevoflurane, a commonly used anesthetic agent, is linked to Gabrg3 through a series of molecular and physiological interactions. The evidence suggests that Sevoflurane, through its inclusion in the class of organofluorine compounds, binds to Gabrg3, a protein involved in anesthetic action. This is further supported by the fact that Sevoflurane causes Breath Holding Attacks, which are associated with Isoflurane, another organofluorine compound that also binds to Gabrg3. The shared ancestry of Sevoflurane and Isoflurane, both belonging to the Anesthetic Agent category, reinforces the notion of a common mechanism in their interaction with Gabrg3. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Gabrg3 in mediating the effects of anesthetics, particularly those in the organofluorine class. The evidence suggests a direct interaction between Sevoflurane and Gabrg3, mediated through the shared structural and functional properties of organofluorine compounds.",
